WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.800
- Now we've all had the experience
of teaching MIT students,

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:08.790
which is new for you.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:12.670
You might have more
insight into it because

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.870
it's fresh on your mind,

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.420
whereas Fox and I have been
doing this for a while,

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.740
and so I'm wondering what
you learn from the students,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.820
and their approach to
computing and creativity

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.660
in different ways,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.010
and how

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.240
they

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.240
share their insights

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:33.120
with you.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.560
- We actually haven't got to this...

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.680
As of timestamped right now,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.480
we haven't got to this section

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.710
where we're gonna do tool-assisted
computational writing.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.350
Everything has been classical approaches

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.450
to writing techniques and stuff like that,

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:52.800
so I haven't seen how they're
gonna be partnering with

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.315
or using technology.

00:00:54.315 --> 00:00:55.290
I haven't had the opportunity to do that.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:59.160
We have talked about different concepts

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.560
in real time as we're doing the class.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.100
You have the rise of

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.890
the AI Jay-Z and

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:13.397
these engines that are capable
of just generating raps.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.310
A little prompt and then it goes.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.850
And so we talk about what, potentially,

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.090
are some of the consequences
of that thinking down the line,

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.010
how that impinges upon them.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:30.070
If they wanted to be, just
casually, rappers as a hobby

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.300
or if they really want to
do this as a profession,

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.550
that is something that you're
gonna have to deal with.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.710
And the interesting part
is that, potentially,

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:38.580
potentially...

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:39.930
We didn't do a survey

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.370
to really dial this in,

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.710
but, potentially, some of
the students in my class

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.720
are probably, unknowingly,

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.470
undoing their rap careers right
now by fine tuning some LLM

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.590
that's gonna put them out of a job

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.550
if they chose to do that.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.260
So I would just say in general,

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.570
so just maybe

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:01.740
stepping out just a little bit away

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.280
from that specific computational aspect,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.730
just the students in general,

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.490
it's a range of people
that are super committed,

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:14.940
super interested, very microscopically,

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.460
about a very specific issue or problem

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:22.200
or theoretical approach to
crafting personification.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.300
Like, "Okay, how can I?"

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.700
Or it's things about their delivery,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.610
just the level of focus
on a particular aspect

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.630
is very interesting.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:31.590
But again, it's the spectrum.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.053
So you have students
that are way on that end,

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.390
and then you have just some students

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:37.822
that are just melling it in.

00:02:37.822 --> 00:02:39.990
(laughs)

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.540
I got to wake them up in class.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.348
- They stayed up all night doing their

00:02:45.348 --> 00:02:46.181
problem sets.
- They stayed up all night

00:02:46.181 --> 00:02:47.580
doing their P sets.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.323
That might be the excuse.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.840
But then you got some that...

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:52.950
It's a range,

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.410
but it's good to be in that level of...

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.830
There's definitely a few that definitely

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:03.090
ask the deep questions
about a specific example

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.060
and want to drag out as much
as the opportunity as possible.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.460
So it's really, It's been,
really, a fascinating

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.060
opportunity to work with them like that.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.650
- And, Fox, what's your perspective?

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.150
'Cause you've also taught classes that are

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.730
extending the work of your
research and studio practices,

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.780
where students come,

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.680
and they're engaged with

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.500
active projects that you're doing.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.200
- Well, I've had a range of
experiences with students.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.150
So at one end...

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:34.470
You could say at one end of the spectrum,

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:38.880
we have students who
are highly computational

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.090
and think in a computational

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:42.923
way.

00:03:42.923 --> 00:03:44.310
They structure their thinking in that way,

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:47.430
which can manifest in forms

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:51.420
when talking about a
subjective, humanities concept,

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.290
looking really confused

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.280
and having to introspect.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.020
And when I ask what's the issue?

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:03.020
They say, "Well, you gave us
one definition of literacy,

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:04.890
and then I read another definition,

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.870
and then the student presenter
has another definition.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.700
So what is the correct definition?"

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:09.880
And I have to explain

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:14.160
that it's really a dialogue
with the ideas and theory

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.300
and developing your own.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.200
And at the other end,

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.670
we do also have students

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.940
who are highly artistic and conceptual.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.567
And I've had those students say,

00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:23.790
"Well, wouldn't it be okay

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.700
if I create the specs
for something or design

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.617
but I don't actually build it?"

00:04:28.617 --> 00:04:30.780
And I say that there's
something that you can gain

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.040
in making too.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.200
I mean, as an artist, you know that,

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.930
and I'd like you to explore
this medium for making

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:39.360
just like the other
media that you've used.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.290
So there's quite a spectrum in that way.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.063
And when I try to help them see...

00:04:45.063 --> 00:04:48.750
It's like my drawing teacher
told me back as a undergrad,

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.930
'cause, as you know, I studied
both logic and computation,

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.250
computer science,

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.290
which I'll put those two on one side,

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:56.820
and then also art,

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.880
but really integrating them
rather than different sides.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.190
And I just...

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:03.520
You could feel

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.520
like it's

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:09.410
really...

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.390
My drawing teacher put it like this,

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:13.590
drawing is thinking

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:16.380
rather than it's just some natural skill.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:17.670
And I think about it that way,

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.470
the building is part of the thinking,

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.870
and the theorizing can
lead to the building.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:23.340
- There's a great book, "Drawing Thought"

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.900
that the MIT Press just published.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.230
I just ran into the author
in a coffee shop in New York.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.140
And it's about

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:33.810
just whether or not
you're trained or skilled

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.460
to draw as an artist.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:36.330
It's a practice

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.210
that slows down your
thinking and perceptions

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.670
so that you can understand them better.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:42.503
And

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.170
I also have had a wide
variety of experiences

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:49.540
with students

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:54.360
and their engagement with
technology and creativity.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.700
I don't particularly find
that students want to do,

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:00.360
like, "Oh, here's the project
that takes more programming.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:04.320
I'll do that instead of
this project that involves

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.260
just a lot of writing and doing something

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:08.880
with a material book form."

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:09.713
Something like that.

00:06:09.713 --> 00:06:12.607
I don't actually see their
inclination as being like,

00:06:12.607 --> 00:06:14.610
"Let's do more digital stuff."

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:18.330
But when they're trying to understand,

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:20.490
for instance, humanistic
and artistic concepts

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.050
about how does narrative work, then

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:25.833
formal approaches,

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:30.390
or if you're trying to
give them some ideas

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:31.560
about how do you write,

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.053
and

00:06:33.053 --> 00:06:34.980
you share with them the
techniques of the oulipo

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:36.840
and constrained writing

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.720
and different sorts of
rules and restrictions

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.300
on how you can write
that are non-traditional,

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.090
students can get very interested in this,

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.560
and seeing how

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.350
the types of thinking
that they can do formally,

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.530
and that they're used
to doing in other areas

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.023
can also apply in the humanities and arts.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:57.390
I think that's it more than...

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:58.680
I mean, there's people I know

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.810
from the first two days
and before of your class

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.150
that they wanna rap about nuclear physics.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.400
They want to tell you about how

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.980
part of their identity is
being a computer science major,

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.157
and like,

00:07:09.157 --> 00:07:11.435
"I'm six three and five two."

00:07:11.435 --> 00:07:13.518
(laughs)

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:16.433
But

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.770
the topic matter,

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:21.120
the subject matter isn't, I think,

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:23.520
as significant as the conceptual approach.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:24.480
- Mm. Mm.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:26.780
I mean, there's a lot of...

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:31.770
What I'm dealing with, to
your point, is less about...

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:34.833
I mean, they realize
they're in the humanities.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.993
And whatever high-tech
course that they're in,

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.180
it's almost like this
is their opportunity to

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.523
just be natural, just be a human being,

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:52.860
and play with those thoughts
and ideas and concepts,

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.390
and express themselves in that way.

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:55.900
So the expectation would be

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.400
that they would be doing all of this

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.520
highly technical structured type stuff.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.810
But also, it comes out
as just very personal,

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:10.740
very opportunity for them
to express themselves.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:14.265
Even though I put a lot
of structured theory

00:08:14.265 --> 00:08:17.240
like Alan Lomax and all these other...

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:20.990
George Lakoff and all these other folks.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:23.130
But even in that.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.751
So they'll take that process that...

00:08:25.751 --> 00:08:26.760
I don't know why we're
talking about students

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:27.660
without them being in the room,

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:28.493
but it's cool.

00:08:28.493 --> 00:08:29.370
Don't worry, we got you.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.353
No names. It's all anonymous.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:34.440
But even through the...

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.683
You fronted with all this
theory and all this structure,

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:41.800
and then they still, they get
through, they absorb that.

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:42.633
And it's like, "Okay, that's cool,

00:08:42.633 --> 00:08:45.330
but I'ma still tell the story of my life

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.040
or the thing that I'm going
through in the moment."

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.470
So it's interesting to see how they

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:50.640
go through that process,

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:51.510
which, you would think,

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:53.010
would structure them in a certain way,

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:54.540
but they still just use it to produce

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:56.700
very abstract things,
which is interesting.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:57.960
Organic things.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.630
- I mean, the reason I
bring the question up,

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:03.420
it's related to what
Chilla Jones IT was saying

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.970
about elite battle rap, that, to him,

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:08.853
it's much more interesting
what the really hungry,

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.640
young people are doing

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:13.473
than the really established people.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:16.150
And you learn from the people

00:09:17.319 --> 00:09:19.860
who have the freedom
to try out new things.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:21.270
And so, I mean...

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:24.610
So I think that's one of
the reasons to look to

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:28.860
what are the undergrads perspective?

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:29.800
Not just because

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:31.523
we are teaching them,

00:09:31.523 --> 00:09:33.170
but also we're learning from them.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:35.400
- I think,

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:36.880
and I'll land on this, is

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:41.250
you're still dealing with...

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.050
That's cool if you're dealing with people

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:46.113
who already came in at a
certain level of expertise.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:49.200
Or that math solution is there,

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.510
you just need to copy it
and that's what it is.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:52.803
And maybe you get it
wrong, you get it right.

00:09:52.803 --> 00:09:54.660
It's different when
they're like, "This is my

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.280
first time rapping,-
- That's right.

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:57.620
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:00.810
- and here's this whole
sleeve of raps that I have,

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.160
and it's not to beat."

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:03.690
I'm just sitting there
like, "Yeah, this is..."

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:04.987
Like,

00:10:04.987 --> 00:10:06.507
"We got to shape this."

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:08.303
But it's been good.

00:10:08.303 --> 00:10:11.640
It's been good seeing the growth
of those types of students.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.620
And their learning curve
increase over time,

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:16.530
and learning small lessons
and then self-correcting

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:19.443
and then producing really
quality work, of a quality.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:21.513
And then even the students that came in

00:10:21.513 --> 00:10:24.483
that were already hitting
on high cylinders,

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.070
interjecting a little bit more
of the basics into their work

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:30.720
is pretty interesting.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:31.619
- Yeah.

00:10:31.619 --> 00:10:33.180
What I would say is, in general,

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:37.800
when using the computer to
create forms of storytelling,

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:39.000
poetry,

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:39.900
rap,

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:40.797
etc.,

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:42.810
people...

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:44.280
We're using formal methods.

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:47.220
We're using mathematical
representations, data structures

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:48.330
and so forth,

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.673
and I think people bring a
lot of baggage with that.

00:10:50.673 --> 00:10:53.850
That means they're used
to, for example, proofs,

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:57.960
that there is just one answer
or you can say if this,

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:01.530
you can verify something
or not and so forth.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.170
And I feel like they bring
those ideas, sometimes,

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:06.750
into the creative artworks too.

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:11.490
Like, "Is this story a
functional story? Does it work?

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.830
Is it as good as a human story or not?"

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:16.530
That's a yes no question.
These sort of things.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:19.620
And I feel like the
questions I want to ask

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:22.930
are much more subjective kind of questions

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:30.120
like what cultural perspective
does this story ask?

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:33.180
How is it using the
imagination and metaphor

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.433
to convey an idea?

00:11:38.332 --> 00:11:42.870
Is it something that can
express the emotional tone

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:46.080
in an ambiguous way that
causes some kind of tension

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.960
in the person who is experiencing it?

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:50.910
And I think it's,
sometimes, hard for people

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:54.270
to reconcile the precise and mathematical

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:56.460
with those kind of questions.
But at the end of the day,

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:59.280
I feel like, as creative practitioners,

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:01.380
using AI and computing,

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:04.830
that's what we're trying to do
often, is provoke reflection,

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:06.690
provoke experience, engagement,

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:08.010
and those sort of things.

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:09.540
Just this happens to be the medium

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.100
through which we are doing it.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:11.933
- Yeah.

00:12:11.933 --> 00:12:15.813
And I would go beyond that and
say from my own standpoint,

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:19.770
can these systems be non-human?

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:21.510
Can they be uncanny?

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:25.050
Can they be something other than we would

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:26.940
but still be resonant,

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:29.880
still actually have implications for us

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:32.133
and still interest us in what they do?

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:36.180
So maybe that's a good way to

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.250
switch over to...

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:40.650
I was telling somebody the other day.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:42.120
I found

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.090
something that I actually hate worse than

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:47.220
GPT-4 generated text,

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:50.830
which is human-written hot takes about

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:52.836
GPT-4.

00:12:52.836 --> 00:12:54.414
(laughs)

00:12:54.414 --> 00:12:56.157
- Hey, GPT-4, he's saying that, not me,

00:12:56.157 --> 00:12:58.635
so when you become sentient, remember.

00:12:58.635 --> 00:13:00.802
(laughs)

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:05.430
- But I'm interested in
your hot takes though,

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:06.263
I have to say.

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:08.963
'Cause we already...

00:13:08.963 --> 00:13:11.220
You already mentioned
large language models

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:13.623
as having implications for creativity.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:17.850
You didn't mention a
particular style of AI

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:20.370
because there's a lot
of historical engagement

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:21.580
previous to

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:24.280
transformer-based

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:26.493
LLMs.

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:30.660
So I'm interested in...

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:31.650
I mean,

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:33.930
we've already talked
about some of the ways,

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:34.763
potentially,

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:37.210
replacing some types of

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:39.903
writing, creativity.

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:42.180
I mean, for that matter.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:43.323
Like illustration.

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:47.380
But then also doing things

00:13:49.057 --> 00:13:52.290
that aren't just straightforward in their

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:56.670
replacement of a human or

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:57.513
being like that.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.620
What are the directions
that interest you the most?

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:03.090
Where do you want to...

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:04.030
When you

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:07.750
get behind the wheel of that

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:10.860
AI or large language model,

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:13.110
carve your name into the dashboard and

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:14.364
take it to Mexico,-

00:14:14.364 --> 00:14:16.447
(laughs)

00:14:18.321 --> 00:14:19.552
- What an image.

00:14:19.552 --> 00:14:21.900
- Yeah. Exactly.
- Wow. What a great image.

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:23.850
- What do you wanna do with it?

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:24.750
- Right.

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:28.530
I mean, if I had to, first
of all, create a metaphor

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:29.720
for what I'd like to do with the system,

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:32.140
it might be something more

00:14:33.945 --> 00:14:36.870
like founding the new

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:38.790
realm of that,

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:40.920
empowers rather than oppress.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:41.753
I don't know.

00:14:41.753 --> 00:14:42.586
Something

00:14:42.586 --> 00:14:43.419
like this.

00:14:43.419 --> 00:14:45.933
Enabled and embedded by this technology.

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:49.500
I would

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:50.333
say...

00:14:50.333 --> 00:14:53.190
I mean, I have different answers
related to social critique

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:55.140
as well, compared to what I would say

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:58.200
for what I want to do with
the technology creatively,

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:01.500
because in terms of social critique,

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.093
I would say things like,

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:06.823
there are a lot of...

00:15:06.823 --> 00:15:08.460
Of course, there are a lot of

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:10.380
issues we have to watch out for

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:13.830
that relates to deep
fakes, disinformation,

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:15.030
I mean, these kind of topics

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:17.280
that come along with synthetic media,

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:19.380
they're also approaches to address it,

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:22.830
whether that's using
cryptography and watermarking

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:25.137
or through public policy.

00:15:25.137 --> 00:15:28.371
- I was just thinking the Popes coat,

00:15:28.371 --> 00:15:30.240
the most recent.
- Right.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:31.380
Right.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:34.410
But what I always say about that is

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:35.880
the fidelity is something new,

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:37.800
and so it's an empirical issue

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:40.680
whether the greater
fidelity is going to be

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:44.580
that much more convincing or
have a transformative impact

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:46.620
along with other factors in society.

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:49.590
Because you can go back
to, say, John Heartfield

00:15:49.590 --> 00:15:51.030
and manipulated photographs

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:52.953
for art being used for propaganda.

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:56.460
You can see that they're manipulated,

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:58.050
but others where you can't.

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:02.010
And then Susan Sontag's
work about photography,

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:05.700
say, "On Photography" about
framing and the choice of frame

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:08.100
and all of these kind of factors

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:11.490
that show that it's
not just a transparent,

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:13.410
truthful medium.

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:15.300
So I am...

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:18.030
I don't think that all
of the issues are new

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:20.070
with that particular medium,

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:22.230
but the sensory fidelity,

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:24.300
yeah, that certainly is new,

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:27.450
but maybe even more than that are the

00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:31.470
vehicles for disseminating
this kind of information,

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:34.560
say, through social media,
mass media being distributed

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:35.393
and so forth.

00:16:35.393 --> 00:16:37.320
And so I think it's really the ecology,

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:40.338
not just the images or the
works that are being created

00:16:40.338 --> 00:16:43.410
that creates the issue there.

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:46.590
One example is I'm co-teaching
a course right now,

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:48.840
and we're talking about
the revolutionary thinker,

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:49.673
Frantz Fanon,

00:16:49.673 --> 00:16:52.800
and my colleague, well our
colleague, Michel DeGraff,

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:55.500
the linguist here, was interested

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:59.130
in what would be generated by ChatGPT-4.

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:01.420
And what was interesting
was it wasn't just

00:17:02.740 --> 00:17:05.190
the information about Frantz Fanon,

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:08.700
the quotes by him were also
generated by the system,

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:09.870
which was quite disturbing

00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:12.030
if you think about the
implications for journalism

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:13.473
and so forth.

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:16.980
In terms of my own creative practice,

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:18.870
it's more the kind of things I mentioned,

00:17:18.870 --> 00:17:22.470
like working collaboratively
with the AI system

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:25.890
to create a more improvisational
approach to the medium

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:30.630
in the same way that, say, a
jazz composer or improviser

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:34.590
creates a framework for a
work that can vary within it.

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:35.820
Or

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:37.770
in some forms of freestyle rapping,

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:39.400
you might have some things that

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:41.813
are memorized,

00:17:41.813 --> 00:17:43.770
but you're also filling
in the blanks each time.

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:44.880
And it can be different each time.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:47.280
And I think there's a lot
of forms of improvisation

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:48.630
that are like that,

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:51.630
where you can compose at the
level of general structure

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:54.930
and then, through practice,
it can be filled in

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:56.460
at the speed of thought,

00:17:56.460 --> 00:17:58.770
or at the speed of computing in this case,

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:01.530
and what could be done
interestingly within that.

00:18:01.530 --> 00:18:04.350
So those are some of the
issues in terms of...

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.370
And in terms of technique,

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:08.940
I think it's interesting that
connectionist approaches,

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:10.800
artificial-neural-network
based approaches,

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:12.393
which were formerly seen,

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:14.790
say, in the '80s, as the ones

00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:18.570
that would emulate the
human neural structure

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:19.460
now we think of

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:22.590
as being very far from
human neural structure

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:25.060
because it's more loosely inspired

00:18:26.003 --> 00:18:27.570
by human neural structure.

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:29.880
And so those approaches-
- And not embodied.

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:30.720
- Right. Yeah, Right.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:31.890
- Most notably.
- Right.

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:34.800
And so those approaches
then were seen as the ones

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.830
which were not expedient
for just getting results.

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:40.620
They're the ones used by
cognoscientists who were interested

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:43.770
in the way that the human mind,
brain body complex worked.

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:45.450
And now it's inverted,

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:47.400
where those are the
approaches that are being used

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:49.290
just to get strong results,

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:51.300
and they're not necessarily being used

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:53.130
because of some perceived fidelity

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:54.360
to human neural structure.

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:55.860
So it's just been interesting for me

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:59.100
to watch the arc of those approaches.

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:01.350
And I know in some of
the work we've discussed

00:19:02.477 --> 00:19:04.080
with generating hip-hop lyrics,

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:05.760
with generating rap and so forth,

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:08.430
it hasn't been focused just on

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:11.010
does this look like what a
human rapper would create,

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:14.430
but more understanding
the cultural processes,

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:17.910
the way that somebody thinks about rap.

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.880
Versus, bars, rhymes and so forth.

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:21.930
And because of that,

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:24.600
we wanted something which
is humanly understandable,

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:25.650
rather than something

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:30.120
that is buried in the statistical
processing of the system.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:31.650
And so it just makes me think

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:34.500
that we need a range of
different kind of AI techniques,

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:35.760
including the symbolic

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:38.070
that are sometimes dismissed as GOFAI,

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:39.630
good old-fashioned AI.

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:41.880
These days we need to leverage

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:43.410
any of these approaches.

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:45.090
And I know our good
friend, Michael Mathias,

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:47.310
talks about that as expressive AI,

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:50.340
which is where you can
use any means necessary

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:53.070
using AI to achieve expressive ends.

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:54.390
And I think that's really right

00:19:54.390 --> 00:19:56.430
for the kind of thing that
we are trying to accomplish.

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:57.263
- Well. Yeah.

00:19:57.263 --> 00:19:59.730
I mean, all models have parameters

00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:01.590
if we're talking about
computational models.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:02.820
LLMs have parameters,

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.850
but the parameters don't
have much to do with...

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:09.030
They have to do with
temperature and top K.

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:14.030
And they're statistical
parameters of language and tokens.

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.520
They're not something to do with

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:21.660
the way that people think when
they freestyle, for instance.

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:22.493
And so

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:25.890
what we've been working on in this project

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:29.550
is to try to make an
explicit model, first off,

00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:31.980
of what's good and what's bad.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:36.063
How do we evaluate something
that's better or worse?

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:39.210
How can we tell

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:42.180
when someone is actually being responsive?

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:44.310
How can we tell when...

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:47.610
How can we start to make better
and better approximations

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:52.473
of when these bars are fire
and these over here are whack?

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:56.730
Why exactly do we think that,

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:58.650
and how can we, how can we focus on that?

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:00.150
And to me,

00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:03.153
one of the things about
responsive improvisational rap,

00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:07.260
one of the terms for
it has been freestyle,

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:11.370
is that it's a practice that's widespread,

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:13.203
originated in the United States.

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:16.110
It's a worldwide practice

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:18.450
that people undertake
in different languages,

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:19.283
and

00:21:20.370 --> 00:21:23.370
it's very lively, verbal art

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:26.130
that really isn't very highly valued,

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:29.220
even including, I'd say
within, for instance,

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:30.750
the music recording industry.

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:32.060
I mean, that's...

00:21:35.050 --> 00:21:36.670
So I'm interested in how these

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:41.670
creative and intriguing
cognitive processes

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:43.770
that people undertake

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:45.900
that are maybe based on
different ways of thinking,

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:49.950
on orality rather than
our hyper literate society

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:50.820
in certain ways.

00:21:50.820 --> 00:21:52.269
Although

00:21:52.269 --> 00:21:54.569
the people who are the
best freestyle rappers,

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:58.890
some of them literally read
the dictionary again and again.

00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:01.983
I mean, so it's not
like they're illiterate,

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:05.220
but there are different ways of thinking.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.350
And we can explore that

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:11.040
by using different types
of AI and modeling.

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:12.600
So, all right.

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:14.100
Now that we've

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:17.190
gone off, Lu IT, what do you think

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:20.250
interest you in terms of

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:22.680
advanced computing technologies

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:24.660
and their engagement with rap?

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:25.950
- But if I can just one thing,

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:26.783
just because

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:32.250
the phrase that rap is
not culturally valued,

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:33.083
I wanna challenge,

00:22:33.083 --> 00:22:35.100
I mean, just in terms of-
- Oh. Not rap.

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:36.510
But I was saying, specifically,

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:39.810
improvisational responsive rap.

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:42.120
That's why people do it on the street

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:42.953
for...

00:22:44.475 --> 00:22:46.740
- It has a, It has a, It has a place.

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:47.573
I mean,

00:22:47.573 --> 00:22:48.406
it's a...

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:51.453
I wouldn't dare call rap a niche,

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:55.800
but in language then everything
how language is used,

00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:57.000
rap is definitely a niche.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:01.560
And then freestyling is a
niche within rap for sure.

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:04.680
And there are spaces
where it is highly valued,

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:06.255
but not in the...

00:23:06.255 --> 00:23:07.500
You could say on the recording side,

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.030
if they're recording it

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:11.040
in terms of the footage
of someone freestyling,

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:12.870
but not in terms of...

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:16.323
There's no big push to
get freestyle albums.

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:19.980
You don't have that being

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:21.930
marketed and produced by major labels

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:23.280
and stuff like that.
- But I didn't mean to say...

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:24.630
I saw the Super Bowl.

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:26.080
I didn't mean to say that rap

00:23:27.810 --> 00:23:29.610
is not culturally valued.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:30.630
I'm not saying that.

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:34.800
I'm saying that it's
specifically this type of

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:36.510
improvisational responsive rap,

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:37.920
which people do,

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:39.240
and they have fun,

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.250
and it takes a certain type of mind to it.

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:45.240
And there's people who
try to, who are YouTubers,

00:23:45.240 --> 00:23:46.500
who

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:47.580
are trying to do their thing.

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:49.650
- That's what I was getting at. There are.

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:51.900
If you can do it well,

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:52.980
then it has

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:57.300
a value, possibly, even above...

00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:58.414
Well,

00:23:58.414 --> 00:23:59.850
I'll put an asterisk on that,

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:03.360
a value even above just a
base-level recording artist

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:04.600
who can just put out

00:24:05.446 --> 00:24:07.260
a milk-toast album every now and again.

00:24:07.260 --> 00:24:10.143
You can have a guy who, on
the internet, on YouTube,

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:15.150
who can just have a
YouTube, one YouTube video

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:16.660
of him or her freestyling

00:24:18.370 --> 00:24:19.860
with prompts and these different things

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:22.230
that'll get more views,

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:26.730
and, potentially, generate more
actual monetary value than,

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:28.860
again, a base-level rapper putting out

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:31.380
just a general album of pre-written,

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:32.730
rehearsed stuff like that.

00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:33.563
So it does...

00:24:33.563 --> 00:24:35.910
If you're really good, then
it becomes highly valued.

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:37.150
And then also just

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:41.430
as one of the spaces that
a rapper moves through,

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:42.263
just this

00:24:42.263 --> 00:24:44.550
training ground.

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:48.660
Freestyling is definitely still held dear,

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:49.740
and

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:52.740
it's almost like a rite of passage.

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:54.870
But then also, there's spaces.

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:56.730
So there's the "Funkmaster Flex Show",

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:58.740
which aren't really freestyles,

00:24:58.740 --> 00:24:59.820
but

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:01.530
you never know. And I
think that's the thing with

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:03.780
the wicked side of freestyling,

00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:05.610
but there are spaces

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:08.970
that are dedicated for you
to come and do the freestyle.

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:13.860
And even in those spaces,
so like Sway, they'll have,

00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:15.570
there are people who freestyle.

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:17.190
They'll be prompted, they'll
hit them with things.

00:25:17.190 --> 00:25:18.487
It's on the radio,

00:25:18.487 --> 00:25:20.700
it's live, etc., it's recorded,

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:23.040
and it's going to keep getting propagated

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:23.873
through social media.

00:25:23.873 --> 00:25:25.470
So it does have a value,

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:26.303
but

00:25:26.303 --> 00:25:29.700
definitely not as general
value as putting out a album,

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:30.533
a recorded album.

00:25:30.533 --> 00:25:32.850
- With regard to the visual
art production, for instance,

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:34.143
using generative models,

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:38.400
I heard a interesting talk from a guy who

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:40.290
works as a concept artist.

00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:41.730
He's an industrial artist.

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:43.140
He has to hand the stuff he does

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:46.170
off to people who do the
3D modeling in Blender,

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:47.422
and then someone does the animation,

00:25:47.422 --> 00:25:49.510
and it goes through this

00:25:50.370 --> 00:25:51.720
production chain.

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:55.020
And he was really fascinated by Midjourney

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:57.150
and learning about artistic styles.

00:25:57.150 --> 00:25:58.380
But he was also really clear.

00:25:58.380 --> 00:25:59.213
He said,

00:25:59.213 --> 00:26:00.750
"I can't use this stuff."

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:03.000
Like, "I can't actually
use the things it produces

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:05.490
because it doesn't do
the anatomy correctly.

00:26:05.490 --> 00:26:10.080
It doesn't do perspective
correctly on architectural

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:10.913
stuff.

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:13.800
I might get an idea from it,

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:17.520
and then I have to
repaint the thing myself."

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:19.563
But if you're a trained artist,

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:23.320
you notice things that people

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:25.890
who just looking at the stuff,

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:28.470
they might be like, "Oh,
that's really cool.",

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:30.420
but you're like, "Wait, this doesn't...

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:33.690
I've been doing this since I was five,

00:26:33.690 --> 00:26:35.100
and I've been to art
school and everything."

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:36.067
It's like,

00:26:36.067 --> 00:26:37.917
"Actually, this doesn't look right."

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:43.200
So I'm wondering about that,

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:44.460
in terms of your perspective

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:46.440
on language and generative models,

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:48.040
and how they work with language,

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:51.820
and being part of

00:26:54.193 --> 00:26:56.910
a system in which you're gonna have beats,

00:26:56.910 --> 00:26:58.810
you're gonna overlay language on those

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:03.090
that's spoken verbally, it's
doesn't come out texturally.

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:05.160
And so how does everything fit together

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:08.223
to make something that
is innovative in the end?

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:11.327
- I think it's a...

00:27:11.327 --> 00:27:12.960
It's definitely a bomb has went off,

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:15.060
and a lot of

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:16.320
things are still in the air.

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:18.417
There's still shrapnel flying,

00:27:18.417 --> 00:27:20.280
and it hasn't landed yet.

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:21.530
It's still moving around.

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:24.000
There's definitely

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:29.400
a lot of arenas that are
being shaken right now,

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:31.950
and being tested and collapsing,

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:33.600
and other things that are being built

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:35.730
out of the rubble of that,

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:37.380
creating these new structures.

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:39.990
I'm always down for that.

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:43.140
I think there's always
revolutionary moments

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:45.600
based in advancements in technology.

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:47.790
That's actually the
core motto of my class.

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:50.880
Every creative decision has an
industrial tandem somewhere,

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:52.110
and every industrial decision

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:53.700
has a creative tandem on the other side.

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:56.043
And where do you fit in that balance.

00:27:57.750 --> 00:28:00.480
And I think part of that
is to create new work,

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:02.370
to create new things

00:28:02.370 --> 00:28:04.113
to make certain things obsolete.

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:06.090
And then

00:28:06.090 --> 00:28:09.720
the folks who can adapt
to it, adapt to it,

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:12.090
and they create things that
have never been seen before,

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:13.500
that have never been heard before,

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:15.513
that weren't possible before.

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:21.180
Imagine being able to layer
20 different writing styles,

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:24.660
and then compressing that into a verse.

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:28.740
But now it's like, oh, now we have to...

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:32.130
part of this verse has to
be recorded audibly, orally,

00:28:32.130 --> 00:28:34.290
and then part of it has
to be done textually,

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:35.820
and then part of it
has to be done visually

00:28:35.820 --> 00:28:38.400
to really get the sense
of all the dimensions

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:41.400
that we weren't just
able to process before.

00:28:41.400 --> 00:28:44.160
Or it took 20 rappers in a room,

00:28:44.160 --> 00:28:46.230
different people trained
in different things

00:28:46.230 --> 00:28:48.690
to bring all these layers to bear.

00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:51.540
And then the challenge is,
well, how do you present that?

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:52.770
So even thinking of art,

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:53.603
and I paint,

00:28:53.603 --> 00:28:54.480
so

00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:55.450
I'm very close

00:28:56.316 --> 00:28:58.350
to that, to the visual side of it as well.

00:28:58.350 --> 00:29:00.120
And just in the process of painting,

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:02.550
to your point earlier, is doing the work.

00:29:02.550 --> 00:29:03.383
There's theory,

00:29:03.383 --> 00:29:05.310
but then when you do it,
things start to change.

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:07.200
Certain things that you thought were

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:10.320
very, very necessary to complete

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:13.890
become completely obsolete
in the process of that.

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:16.380
So a lot of painting is site specific.

00:29:16.380 --> 00:29:19.140
So it's the light in your studio,

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:23.040
and you saw it at a time
when the oil was halfway dry

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:24.483
and it was pre varnished,

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:27.150
then when you get into a museum,

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:28.770
it's a completely different thing.

00:29:28.770 --> 00:29:31.530
And you have to do all this
resetting all over again.

00:29:31.530 --> 00:29:34.860
So now you have to reset
the light in a certain way.

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:36.960
People have to view it from
a certain vantage point.

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:38.790
You were able to see it
very microscopically,

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:41.820
now they got to see it
macroscopically, etc.

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:44.190
How does it translate into
a flat photo in a book,

00:29:44.190 --> 00:29:45.023
and the way that...

00:29:45.023 --> 00:29:46.860
Just all the different ways
that people consume it,

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:49.230
and are gonna have to
consume it and appreciate it.

00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:51.510
So there's people who will
never see these paintings,

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:53.490
who'll never see these works

00:29:53.490 --> 00:29:54.750
in person,

00:29:54.750 --> 00:29:57.360
and they only can experience
it in a secondary medium,

00:29:57.360 --> 00:29:58.960
a tertiary medium down the line.

00:30:00.537 --> 00:30:01.420
And so I think

00:30:02.310 --> 00:30:04.983
we're experiencing maybe
a little bit of that.

00:30:06.630 --> 00:30:09.810
The blow up is part of now a rap song,

00:30:09.810 --> 00:30:11.860
what does it mean to have a rap song now?

00:30:13.890 --> 00:30:18.210
How valued does the
performative, real part of it?

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:19.114
So

00:30:19.114 --> 00:30:23.850
it's not enough to say we
experience rap through catalog

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:25.500
for the majority of it.

00:30:25.500 --> 00:30:28.380
Well, the majority of its
history in the middle part.

00:30:28.380 --> 00:30:30.030
Originally if you wanted to hear rap song,

00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:32.040
you had to go see it live.

00:30:32.040 --> 00:30:33.900
And then there's a period
where recording comes in,

00:30:33.900 --> 00:30:35.460
all this technology...

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:37.090
Same way if you wanna say

00:30:38.361 --> 00:30:42.360
the access to the studio or
studio, multi-track recording

00:30:42.360 --> 00:30:45.360
or what have you, Pro
Tools, is AI of that moment

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:46.610
coming in and disrupting.

00:30:47.610 --> 00:30:49.890
And you get a lot of recorded music.

00:30:49.890 --> 00:30:51.720
And that was the way people
experienced it, consumed it.

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:54.180
Those were the metrics that
people created through,

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:55.860
were to be recorded.

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:58.620
Now we're in a space
where it's like, okay,

00:30:58.620 --> 00:31:01.440
what happens when recording
becomes completely autonomous,

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:04.050
becomes almost completely unnecessary,

00:31:04.050 --> 00:31:06.300
and you have to go back to
the performative aspect?

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:08.460
So it's almost like
the reverse of the art.

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:10.293
You experience art through catalog,

00:31:11.790 --> 00:31:13.680
but to really experience,
to really see it,

00:31:13.680 --> 00:31:15.240
you got to go see it in person.

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:17.006
But the majority of people
aren't gonna see it in person.

00:31:17.006 --> 00:31:17.970
They're gonna see it in catalog.

00:31:17.970 --> 00:31:19.860
Now we're flip flopping.
- And there's

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:24.300
the idea of the album also, with Spotify,

00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:25.890
is

00:31:25.890 --> 00:31:27.930
becoming who knows what.

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:30.030
I mean, people still release albums.

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:32.100
- Blowing up. Yeah.
- You do.

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:32.940
But

00:31:32.940 --> 00:31:35.910
it's also the three minute song

00:31:35.910 --> 00:31:38.520
because that's what the Spotify algorithm

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:39.483
is gonna privilege.

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:42.690
And not only do you release albums,

00:31:42.690 --> 00:31:44.880
but you also go on tours that

00:31:44.880 --> 00:31:47.400
involve you doing an entire album.

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:48.296
- But then what...

00:31:48.296 --> 00:31:49.129
So then it becomes,

00:31:49.129 --> 00:31:51.390
okay, everybody moves into the real space,

00:31:51.390 --> 00:31:52.680
the performative space,

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:53.940
and they have all this competition.

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:55.560
There's only so many venues.

00:31:55.560 --> 00:31:57.180
There's all these other constraints.

00:31:57.180 --> 00:31:58.590
And it's like, "Well,

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:01.390
now how do I make my performance
that much more better?"

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:04.290
So I think there's opportunity in it

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:08.068
to better

00:32:08.068 --> 00:32:10.860
the quality of the craft
in certain pockets,

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:11.850
certain areas.

00:32:11.850 --> 00:32:13.080
In other areas,

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:15.510
it's gonna become somewhat
obsolete or unnecessary

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:17.640
'cause it's gonna be super saturated.

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:19.800
The other side of the
coin I'm interested in,

00:32:19.800 --> 00:32:21.510
that's just more theoretical,

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:24.930
maybe prognosticating future
events and hoping that happens,

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:25.763
hoping it doesn't.

00:32:25.763 --> 00:32:27.450
But the one thing I definitely want to see

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:29.073
is the tools that spin out.

00:32:31.410 --> 00:32:34.380
What suite of chisels and hammers

00:32:34.380 --> 00:32:37.740
and saws and stuff do I
have now to go and process

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:38.760
and work on this verse,

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:42.513
and what are some things that
I can let the AI take care of?

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:44.610
And what are some things

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:48.213
that I wanna preserve
for me to do by hand?

00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:50.170
So I'm interested in

00:32:51.238 --> 00:32:53.040
the suite of tools that'll spin out of.

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:56.490
- I mean, there's also
a lot more beyond AI.

00:32:56.490 --> 00:32:59.700
Even to go back in time and technology,

00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:02.130
Panasonic makes this Technics turntable

00:33:02.130 --> 00:33:05.610
that they didn't intend for it to be used

00:33:05.610 --> 00:33:09.360
by DJs to isolate the break
beat and allow for rapping,

00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:10.380
but,

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:11.580
there it is.

00:33:11.580 --> 00:33:13.080
We find out that it's durable enough

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:15.120
that you can stick it on top of a speaker,

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:17.880
and it, I mean, it works this way.

00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:19.830
So there's this particular
piece of technology

00:33:19.830 --> 00:33:23.220
that ends up being subverted
essentially for this other use.

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:25.710
And that factors into
what rap is historically.

00:33:25.710 --> 00:33:28.590
- And hip-hop culture too,
to your point earlier,

00:33:28.590 --> 00:33:32.790
this culture is all about
remixing, adaptation,

00:33:32.790 --> 00:33:35.130
taking things and putting
it in places and niches

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:37.590
where it didn't belong,
wasn't expected to be,

00:33:37.590 --> 00:33:39.840
and then producing all of this great,

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:41.010
valuable work out of it.

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:42.030
So that's the other piece,

00:33:42.030 --> 00:33:43.530
how are the rapper,

00:33:43.530 --> 00:33:45.870
how's the rapper mind gonna respond

00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:48.183
to being in this world of AI?

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:50.760
- And that's one of the...

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:52.470
Then if you look at just

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:56.280
the different production channels
and distribution channels,

00:33:56.280 --> 00:33:57.780
from YouTube

00:33:57.780 --> 00:33:59.040
to

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:00.275
other services,

00:34:00.275 --> 00:34:04.020
the reason that you can
be successful financially

00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:05.760
in a certain way as a freestyler

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:07.410
is because there is YouTube.

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:08.860
Otherwise you wouldn't do it.

00:34:10.394 --> 00:34:12.960
And similarly, there's people...

00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:14.617
One time this guy was like,

00:34:14.617 --> 00:34:16.560
"Oh, rap is an American thing,

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:18.246
it's not a worldwide phenomenon."

00:34:18.246 --> 00:34:20.370
And I was like, "What
are you talking about?"

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:22.110
And he said like, "There's nobody-"

00:34:22.110 --> 00:34:23.550
- And then you freestyle battled him?

00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:24.840
- No.

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:26.377
He said, "There's nobody who raps in..."

00:34:26.377 --> 00:34:28.230
"There's nobody who raps in Greenland."

00:34:28.230 --> 00:34:30.630
So I was like, I was like,
"Well that's easy to deal with."

00:34:30.630 --> 00:34:31.860
Just go on YouTube,

00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:33.930
and there's a rapper in Greenland.

00:34:33.930 --> 00:34:35.550
He's talking about...

00:34:35.550 --> 00:34:36.480
He's

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:39.220
rapping in an indigenous
language, talking about how

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:45.870
colonialism from Denmark
and the stereotypes are

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:48.930
oppressive to him and

00:34:48.930 --> 00:34:49.863
his people.

00:34:51.337 --> 00:34:52.590
And so it's actually...

00:34:52.590 --> 00:34:55.770
It's not just parroting
the American media,

00:34:55.770 --> 00:34:57.150
but again, that,

00:34:57.150 --> 00:34:59.490
you wouldn't have access to that

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:01.620
if you didn't have network computers,

00:35:01.620 --> 00:35:05.430
the ability to produce the
video in the first place.

00:35:05.430 --> 00:35:07.530
So there's a lot of things

00:35:07.530 --> 00:35:11.820
beyond generative models that
are happening technologically

00:35:11.820 --> 00:35:14.340
that allow for all these sorts
of different engagements.

00:35:14.340 --> 00:35:15.970
And I think of

00:35:17.370 --> 00:35:19.380
our colleague, Ian Condry,

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:21.120
who has this book on Japanese hip-hop,

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:24.300
and he has this diagram of a pyramid,

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:26.490
you could also think of it as an iceberg,

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:27.450
where there's a few people,

00:35:27.450 --> 00:35:29.160
you're on the top of the iceberg.

00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:30.540
You're above the,

00:35:30.540 --> 00:35:32.370
You're above the waterline,

00:35:32.370 --> 00:35:34.030
but there's so much going on

00:35:35.550 --> 00:35:39.690
that people are playing in
clubs or people are just

00:35:39.690 --> 00:35:41.820
freestyling in their living room or

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:43.833
on the street or whatever else.

00:35:45.644 --> 00:35:49.170
And so that stuff underneath the surface,

00:35:49.170 --> 00:35:53.130
being able to be in
communication and be out

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:55.440
is also really intriguing to me.

00:35:55.440 --> 00:35:58.230
And those are things involved
with computing technologies,

00:35:58.230 --> 00:36:00.010
whether or not they're

00:36:01.440 --> 00:36:06.300
super sophisticated innovations
in transformer-based LLMs.

00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:09.630
- Yeah. There are so many
threads that have been unpacked.

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:13.140
So if I can go back through
them, I guess, one at a time.

00:36:13.140 --> 00:36:16.170
So in terms of hip-hop

00:36:16.170 --> 00:36:18.760
culture and rap music being valued,

00:36:18.760 --> 00:36:21.430
well, I thought you were referring to more

00:36:22.890 --> 00:36:27.240
capital C culture, lowercase
C culture kind of divide.

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:29.640
When it first came out and people...

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:31.110
were early '80s.

00:36:31.110 --> 00:36:33.870
I mean, it came out in the
early, I mean, in the '70s,

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:35.880
but then in the early '80s,

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:38.077
in the news you'd hear
these sort of things like,

00:36:38.077 --> 00:36:39.240
"Is it really music?"

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:41.190
I mean, just ridiculous

00:36:41.190 --> 00:36:42.023
arguments.

00:36:43.080 --> 00:36:43.980
So yeah.

00:36:43.980 --> 00:36:47.100
But I understand that's
not really your focus.

00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:49.080
Then we moved into discussing the value

00:36:49.080 --> 00:36:51.030
of freestyle.

00:36:51.030 --> 00:36:53.400
And that means both

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:56.250
the way it's culturally
esteemed in general

00:36:56.250 --> 00:37:00.660
within various hip-hop
communities and economically

00:37:00.660 --> 00:37:01.493
too

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:06.510
by so many different criteria.

00:37:06.510 --> 00:37:08.880
And I thought it's not just a matter

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:11.490
of the platforms that we have now.

00:37:11.490 --> 00:37:13.320
'Cause if I think back to, say...

00:37:13.320 --> 00:37:16.110
We were speaking about
undergraduates earlier,

00:37:16.110 --> 00:37:18.270
when I was an undergraduate,

00:37:18.270 --> 00:37:21.060
There was one, there was
a hip-hop album and rapper

00:37:21.060 --> 00:37:22.290
that I liked a lot,

00:37:22.290 --> 00:37:26.340
which was Saafir and his
album "Boxcar Sessions".

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:27.900
And I was living in the Bay area,

00:37:27.900 --> 00:37:29.640
so I would see people from...

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:31.500
His crew was called Hobo Junction.

00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:33.120
So I mean, they were just selling

00:37:33.120 --> 00:37:36.030
their mix tapes and
things just on the street,

00:37:36.030 --> 00:37:36.863
and the way...

00:37:36.863 --> 00:37:39.600
I mean, so he, I mean, he had a CD out,

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:42.780
and I mean, was relatively popular,

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:45.330
but not mainstream popular.

00:37:45.330 --> 00:37:47.850
But what we began to hear was

00:37:47.850 --> 00:37:50.850
an apocryphal story about Saafir

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:55.170
versus the entire Hieroglyphics
crew on some radio show

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:56.430
and what happened there.

00:37:56.430 --> 00:37:58.980
And I mean, I'll leave it to
people to go look that up,

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:00.423
look that up themselves.

00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:03.720
And I mean, actually esteemed

00:38:03.720 --> 00:38:06.450
both Hobo Junction and the Hieroglyphics,

00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:07.283
but

00:38:08.280 --> 00:38:10.470
it was interesting,
particularly as someone

00:38:10.470 --> 00:38:13.530
that appreciated Saafir's recorded work,

00:38:13.530 --> 00:38:16.590
but then hearing the story
about what he was capable of

00:38:16.590 --> 00:38:18.420
in terms of freestyle.

00:38:18.420 --> 00:38:21.750
That was something that I
felt for the people I knew

00:38:21.750 --> 00:38:25.650
that increased the value of
everything that he was doing

00:38:25.650 --> 00:38:29.550
by knowing the full range of abilities.

00:38:29.550 --> 00:38:31.320
In terms of

00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:32.290
generating

00:38:33.150 --> 00:38:35.400
content, regardless of what kind it is,

00:38:35.400 --> 00:38:39.210
we spoke about visual arts,
we spoke about rap and more,

00:38:39.210 --> 00:38:40.430
I think about...

00:38:41.460 --> 00:38:45.810
We've spoken about the Argentine
writer, Jorge Luis Borges,

00:38:45.810 --> 00:38:50.070
and the story, "Pierre Menard"
is one that comes to mind.

00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:52.020
So for people who aren't familiar,

00:38:52.020 --> 00:38:55.860
this is a story of somebody who
is writing what he thinks of

00:38:55.860 --> 00:39:00.750
as the great literary work
of the time, in a way.

00:39:00.750 --> 00:39:03.330
And it ends up being "Don Quixote",

00:39:03.330 --> 00:39:06.030
not because "Don Quixote"
was, in its own time,

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:07.830
a great work, it was as if

00:39:07.830 --> 00:39:10.770
he was just conceiving of it at that time

00:39:10.770 --> 00:39:12.300
with no reference to the prior work.

00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:14.520
And it meant something
completely different.

00:39:14.520 --> 00:39:16.290
Because if somebody wrote that book now,

00:39:16.290 --> 00:39:17.730
it would mean something totally different

00:39:17.730 --> 00:39:19.980
than it meant at that time period.

00:39:19.980 --> 00:39:21.600
And so he was just thinking

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:23.400
how groundbreaking it was.

00:39:23.400 --> 00:39:26.550
And I think generated rap is like that.

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:28.530
If you had some generated...

00:39:28.530 --> 00:39:30.720
I mean, generated content in general,

00:39:30.720 --> 00:39:32.190
like the artist you mentioned

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:33.670
who had been training

00:39:34.650 --> 00:39:35.680
in some

00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:40.890
art historically grounded way

00:39:40.890 --> 00:39:43.410
according to certain
traditions since youth.

00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:45.450
And if that person were to violate

00:39:45.450 --> 00:39:48.630
some assumption made
in figurative artwork,

00:39:48.630 --> 00:39:51.720
then people would say that is
intentional and for a reason.

00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:53.850
And maybe that's a deviation

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:55.950
for what they've done in the
past that people didn't like,

00:39:55.950 --> 00:39:58.660
but maybe it's something like, look at

00:40:00.532 --> 00:40:02.283
this new development in their work.

00:40:03.480 --> 00:40:05.070
And I've seen the same thing happen

00:40:05.070 --> 00:40:06.750
with digitally created work,

00:40:06.750 --> 00:40:09.840
even in some of my own
digitally created poetry

00:40:09.840 --> 00:40:11.940
when I was in graduate school.

00:40:11.940 --> 00:40:14.190
If people thought a computer generated it,

00:40:14.190 --> 00:40:15.023
they would think,

00:40:15.023 --> 00:40:16.020
and there was repetition,

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:18.300
they would think there must
be a flaw in the algorithm

00:40:18.300 --> 00:40:20.250
because it's repeating the same thing.

00:40:20.250 --> 00:40:22.950
If you have something like
Edgar Allan Poe's "The Raven",

00:40:22.950 --> 00:40:23.783
people don't think

00:40:23.783 --> 00:40:26.490
that "Nevermore" is just
a bug in the system.

00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:29.790
I mean, it's intrinsic to
the structure of the poem.

00:40:29.790 --> 00:40:30.990
And I think it's like that.

00:40:30.990 --> 00:40:35.270
If you had something where
you had a rapper that...

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:38.310
Such and such rapper

00:40:38.310 --> 00:40:41.250
that's known for doing
innovative things, uses AI

00:40:41.250 --> 00:40:42.960
to create their works,

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:44.940
then people might take it in one way.

00:40:44.940 --> 00:40:47.940
If somebody is known for doing,

00:40:47.940 --> 00:40:48.773
say,

00:40:50.450 --> 00:40:52.080
what people think of as highly commercial,

00:40:52.080 --> 00:40:53.490
almost disposable kind of work,

00:40:53.490 --> 00:40:54.323
and someone said,

00:40:54.323 --> 00:40:56.400
"You know what? Their
rhymes are generated by AI."

00:40:56.400 --> 00:40:58.207
People might say like,
"But that's horrible.

00:40:58.207 --> 00:41:00.567
Why are they just, Why
are they just doing that?"

00:41:00.567 --> 00:41:02.700
And it could be the exact same verse,

00:41:02.700 --> 00:41:05.250
but people take it in a
totally different way.

00:41:05.250 --> 00:41:08.010
So I just think it's
the setting, the person,

00:41:08.010 --> 00:41:11.550
all those things play a role
in how things are being taken.

00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:13.630
So in our task of creating

00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:16.950
rap lyrics that

00:41:16.950 --> 00:41:20.100
we say what would be highly valued,

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:23.310
it's a major challenge to
say under what criteria,

00:41:23.310 --> 00:41:24.270
under which settings

00:41:24.270 --> 00:41:26.100
and so forth.

00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:28.053
I mean, just to give one more example.

00:41:30.702 --> 00:41:31.762
And I think (indistinct)

00:41:31.762 --> 00:41:32.970
you could say more about it,

00:41:32.970 --> 00:41:34.860
but I think there's some
different philosophies.

00:41:34.860 --> 00:41:36.480
I know some

00:41:36.480 --> 00:41:37.593
underground hip-hop,

00:41:38.610 --> 00:41:39.960
where it's

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:43.260
almost like the MIT students,
that Nick mentioned earlier,

00:41:43.260 --> 00:41:46.530
were having as many syllables as possible

00:41:46.530 --> 00:41:48.777
and obscure references
and making it all work,

00:41:48.777 --> 00:41:49.610
all that.

00:41:49.610 --> 00:41:50.760
And that's why

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:52.980
they think like, "Wow, that
person's a really great rapper."

00:41:52.980 --> 00:41:55.740
And then there's the moves the crowd idea.

00:41:55.740 --> 00:41:56.817
And then you say like, "What?"

00:41:56.817 --> 00:41:58.740
And then some people do both.

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:01.530
And then it's hard to
say under which criteria,

00:42:01.530 --> 00:42:03.810
what makes somebody

00:42:03.810 --> 00:42:05.370
the best in a way.

00:42:05.370 --> 00:42:07.350
It really depends under which criteria,

00:42:07.350 --> 00:42:09.090
what they're trying to
do, what the audience...

00:42:09.090 --> 00:42:10.800
I mean, there's so many factors.

00:42:10.800 --> 00:42:13.350
And so, to me, that's what
the "Pierre Menard" story

00:42:13.350 --> 00:42:16.830
is a parable of, is that phenomenon,

00:42:16.830 --> 00:42:19.830
how much context and culture.

00:42:19.830 --> 00:42:22.590
I mean, all those things matter
for how we interpret works.

00:42:22.590 --> 00:42:23.423
- Yeah.

00:42:23.423 --> 00:42:25.380
I mean, when you mention about

00:42:25.380 --> 00:42:28.890
maybe this deviation from good art

00:42:28.890 --> 00:42:30.540
is something interesting

00:42:30.540 --> 00:42:32.910
reminds me to paraphrase Picasso,

00:42:32.910 --> 00:42:35.760
children know how to draw
like a child already.

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:38.550
The problem is getting adults to do that.

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:41.070
How can adults learn to do that?

00:42:41.070 --> 00:42:42.960
But I guess there's something about

00:42:42.960 --> 00:42:45.270
the child-like perspective on the world,

00:42:45.270 --> 00:42:46.200
and

00:42:46.200 --> 00:42:48.693
drawing like a child,
whereas the question is,

00:42:49.770 --> 00:42:51.570
there is an issue,

00:42:51.570 --> 00:42:53.340
is what the generative model

00:42:53.340 --> 00:42:55.920
is doing as a writer, for instance,

00:42:55.920 --> 00:42:57.510
is that really interesting

00:42:57.510 --> 00:43:00.010
because it's being like a very assertive

00:43:02.490 --> 00:43:04.540
plagiaristic high school essayist

00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:07.845
making up the quotations

00:43:07.845 --> 00:43:09.570
from someone and making up citations.

00:43:09.570 --> 00:43:10.403
And it's like...

00:43:12.540 --> 00:43:15.900
I mean, the thing that I
think is interesting about...

00:43:15.900 --> 00:43:17.910
One of the things that I think
is potentially interesting

00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:19.060
about generative models

00:43:20.010 --> 00:43:22.590
and large language models is that

00:43:22.590 --> 00:43:25.860
you can tell something about

00:43:25.860 --> 00:43:28.680
all of the language they've ingested.

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:31.470
It's like the surrealist wanted to get

00:43:31.470 --> 00:43:32.760
into their own unconscious.

00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:34.260
They wanted to do automatic writing,

00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:35.970
and they wanted to surface

00:43:35.970 --> 00:43:38.070
some of the things that they wouldn't have

00:43:38.940 --> 00:43:40.530
written

00:43:40.530 --> 00:43:42.360
in normal circumstances.

00:43:42.360 --> 00:43:43.830
And so maybe these models

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:46.830
are a way of surfacing the
collective unconscious,

00:43:46.830 --> 00:43:49.860
but the super ego

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:54.180
of ChatGPT's reinforcement
training is beating that down

00:43:54.180 --> 00:43:57.543
so that we'd actually don't
have anything offensive.

00:43:58.410 --> 00:44:00.210
The system has to tell you what it knows

00:44:00.210 --> 00:44:01.460
and what it doesn't know.

00:44:02.550 --> 00:44:04.340
So it's actually the...

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:06.083
I just

00:44:06.083 --> 00:44:07.249
want

00:44:07.249 --> 00:44:09.737
the language model that's
pure id if we're gonna,

00:44:09.737 --> 00:44:10.570
(laughs)

00:44:10.570 --> 00:44:11.403
if we're gonna find out

00:44:11.403 --> 00:44:13.181
about this type of stuff.
- Evil GPT?

00:44:13.181 --> 00:44:14.014
- Yeah.

00:44:14.014 --> 00:44:15.060
Well, that's the thing.

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:16.230
I mean, people also...

00:44:16.230 --> 00:44:18.720
There's prompt-injection
attacks and there's

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:20.310
how do we

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:21.960
do anything now and all these.

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:23.920
So I think that's one of the things, is

00:44:24.900 --> 00:44:28.120
what you do with these models as an artist

00:44:29.400 --> 00:44:31.360
may or may not align with the

00:44:33.030 --> 00:44:36.123
interest and plans of the
people who made the models.

00:44:36.990 --> 00:44:38.010
Because,

00:44:38.010 --> 00:44:39.720
to some extent, it's like we want to build

00:44:39.720 --> 00:44:42.210
a better customer service representative.

00:44:42.210 --> 00:44:43.173
That's the goal.

00:44:45.480 --> 00:44:47.230
Or something to entertain you

00:44:48.570 --> 00:44:49.403
online.

00:44:51.007 --> 00:44:53.760
And so maybe that's not the

00:44:53.760 --> 00:44:56.520
idea of tapping into the
collective unconscious

00:44:56.520 --> 00:44:58.953
and doing something that's really radical.

00:44:59.940 --> 00:45:02.250
- Well, I think it's a matter of being

00:45:02.250 --> 00:45:03.990
culturally responsive.

00:45:03.990 --> 00:45:05.520
And so for example,

00:45:05.520 --> 00:45:09.300
that's what made Duchamp's
readymades interesting,

00:45:09.300 --> 00:45:10.950
that is

00:45:10.950 --> 00:45:12.060
if

00:45:12.060 --> 00:45:15.057
an AI system, you wanted to
create some great artwork,

00:45:15.057 --> 00:45:17.250
and then it brings you a bicycle tire

00:45:17.250 --> 00:45:18.330
or something like that,

00:45:18.330 --> 00:45:21.000
you might just think it's
a failure of the system.

00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:22.290
But if it's something

00:45:22.290 --> 00:45:25.320
that would scandalize the
art world at that time

00:45:25.320 --> 00:45:26.910
in a particular kind of way...

00:45:26.910 --> 00:45:28.890
Or to give a personal example,

00:45:28.890 --> 00:45:30.840
when I was in art school,

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:32.790
and I had a very
traditional drawing teacher

00:45:32.790 --> 00:45:34.920
who thought you needed to learn

00:45:34.920 --> 00:45:36.660
the fundamentals of figurative drawing,

00:45:36.660 --> 00:45:37.830
and then you can do

00:45:37.830 --> 00:45:39.840
anything, conceptual artwork,
other sort of things,

00:45:39.840 --> 00:45:42.780
but you thought you should
know the fundamentals first

00:45:42.780 --> 00:45:45.000
and then do other sort of things.

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:46.680
And I actually quite liked his class,

00:45:46.680 --> 00:45:48.330
so it's not just putting
down that approach,

00:45:48.330 --> 00:45:49.470
it's just one approach,

00:45:49.470 --> 00:45:50.820
but

00:45:50.820 --> 00:45:55.590
the point here is when
doing, say, renaissance-style

00:45:55.590 --> 00:45:58.830
drawing and perspective
and lighting and shading

00:45:58.830 --> 00:46:01.260
and the push pull of light
and dark, (indistinct),

00:46:01.260 --> 00:46:03.090
all these techniques.

00:46:03.090 --> 00:46:05.490
And I remember doing a
portrait in that style,

00:46:05.490 --> 00:46:09.000
and a graduate student who
didn't share that point of view

00:46:09.000 --> 00:46:10.097
that looks at the works.

00:46:10.097 --> 00:46:12.210
And so what I did was intentionally...

00:46:12.210 --> 00:46:13.500
Because

00:46:13.500 --> 00:46:15.990
the questions I would
get were the ones, say,

00:46:15.990 --> 00:46:17.520
playing a role-playing game.

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:20.430
And if I were to play, say,
a fantasy role-playing game,

00:46:20.430 --> 00:46:23.460
and I always play as a
Legolas type character.

00:46:23.460 --> 00:46:24.960
People say, "But you're not like that.

00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:26.130
Why are you playing that character?

00:46:26.130 --> 00:46:27.390
Do you wanna have long blonde hair?

00:46:27.390 --> 00:46:29.190
Do you wanna look that way?"

00:46:29.190 --> 00:46:30.450
That sort of thing.

00:46:30.450 --> 00:46:31.410
And yeah.

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:32.827
So they're thinking like,

00:46:32.827 --> 00:46:35.490
"What is my role in society?
How does that relate to me?

00:46:35.490 --> 00:46:38.010
Is it a active imagination
that I could play

00:46:38.010 --> 00:46:40.110
as that character?

00:46:40.110 --> 00:46:42.210
Is it something, Is it self-hatred?

00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:43.454
What is it? What's going on?"

00:46:43.454 --> 00:46:44.580
And so it was like that

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:47.160
using renaissance-style
drawing when I did it

00:46:47.160 --> 00:46:49.680
compared to, say, if we had an
exchange student from Italy,

00:46:49.680 --> 00:46:51.630
it would be seen in a
totally different way.

00:46:51.630 --> 00:46:53.310
So I thought, "Okay,
well, I'm gonna go all in,

00:46:53.310 --> 00:46:55.500
and I'll draw myself in
renaissance apparel.",

00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:56.940
and all these things,

00:46:56.940 --> 00:46:58.110
and make it really obvious.

00:46:58.110 --> 00:46:59.310
And then that question would come,

00:46:59.310 --> 00:47:00.870
and people say, "Why are you doing this?"

00:47:00.870 --> 00:47:02.760
That seems so Eurocentric."

00:47:02.760 --> 00:47:04.080
And I'm like, "Well, that's the point."

00:47:04.080 --> 00:47:05.430
It's like I'm responding to

00:47:07.062 --> 00:47:08.820
what the education is showing us,

00:47:08.820 --> 00:47:11.280
and what is my position in it.

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:13.080
I have to negotiate it in some way.

00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:17.490
So that was responding
intentionally to what I was doing.

00:47:17.490 --> 00:47:20.040
If a system just developed

00:47:20.040 --> 00:47:22.920
that was putting everybody
in renaissance clothes

00:47:22.920 --> 00:47:26.010
no matter what, then it
would be less interesting

00:47:26.010 --> 00:47:28.950
than my intentional
decision as a subversive act

00:47:28.950 --> 00:47:31.050
to draw myself in that way

00:47:31.050 --> 00:47:32.940
because of the questions I was getting.

00:47:32.940 --> 00:47:35.878
- Actually, that sold for a
lot of money at Christie's.

00:47:35.878 --> 00:47:38.045
(laughs)

00:47:40.830 --> 00:47:43.350
- It could be responsive
to this cultural moment.

00:47:43.350 --> 00:47:45.240
So it just depends on whether it is,

00:47:45.240 --> 00:47:46.620
or whether the story about it

00:47:46.620 --> 00:47:49.500
is responsive to that cultural moment.

00:47:49.500 --> 00:47:50.760
- That's where my mind is.

00:47:50.760 --> 00:47:52.380
I think the story about it.

00:47:52.380 --> 00:47:55.950
I think the meta-narrative around, even as

00:47:55.950 --> 00:47:57.200
we're doing it right now,

00:47:59.190 --> 00:48:03.033
will dictate how it's
appreciated, its use cases.

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:06.720
We may even just completely
forget about what it does.

00:48:06.720 --> 00:48:08.070
It could just be sitting in a corner,

00:48:08.070 --> 00:48:11.340
just generating the same
or just a continuous stream

00:48:11.340 --> 00:48:12.420
of what have you,

00:48:12.420 --> 00:48:13.253
of

00:48:14.190 --> 00:48:15.153
neuronal thought.

00:48:16.410 --> 00:48:18.540
But I think it's more

00:48:18.540 --> 00:48:21.480
how are the critics gonna talk about it?

00:48:21.480 --> 00:48:22.313
How are the,

00:48:22.313 --> 00:48:24.180
How do the artists

00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:26.790
annotate their own work and
position their own work?

00:48:26.790 --> 00:48:27.870
Even in my class,

00:48:27.870 --> 00:48:31.620
there's a balance, where
I'm more interested

00:48:31.620 --> 00:48:36.620
in what you think about your
own work than the work itself.

00:48:37.440 --> 00:48:39.691
All of the processes

00:48:39.691 --> 00:48:42.420
and the steps you took to get to the work,

00:48:42.420 --> 00:48:43.710
and your ability to explain it.

00:48:43.710 --> 00:48:45.810
So I'm like, what's more valuable?

00:48:45.810 --> 00:48:48.000
Yeah, your ability to
perform it is one thing,

00:48:48.000 --> 00:48:50.590
but, again, it could be a bicycle tire

00:48:51.540 --> 00:48:53.250
and all that that entails.

00:48:53.250 --> 00:48:54.930
But I want to know what it entails.

00:48:54.930 --> 00:48:57.792
And I think that's where the value,

00:48:57.792 --> 00:48:58.692
a lot of the value

00:48:59.670 --> 00:49:01.560
of AI and generative models is gonna be,

00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:03.960
is the stories that we position

00:49:03.960 --> 00:49:05.610
around the things that it creates

00:49:05.610 --> 00:49:07.380
in addition to the story
that we talk about,

00:49:07.380 --> 00:49:10.380
the technology itself, even if
the technology didn't exist.

00:49:10.380 --> 00:49:11.313
'Cause we were...

00:49:11.313 --> 00:49:13.050
There were stories around AI

00:49:13.050 --> 00:49:15.630
before AI even existed in a way

00:49:15.630 --> 00:49:18.120
for us to really participate in it.

00:49:18.120 --> 00:49:21.291
So I think Skynet and the
Terminator and stuff like that.

00:49:21.291 --> 00:49:23.340
(laughs)

00:49:23.340 --> 00:49:24.398
And

00:49:24.398 --> 00:49:25.740
it's almost like,

00:49:25.740 --> 00:49:27.030
it's almost like a casino thing.

00:49:27.030 --> 00:49:29.040
I think some people are gambling

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:32.700
just long bets about my
story's gonna be the story

00:49:32.700 --> 00:49:34.410
that's gonna be the true one about AI.

00:49:34.410 --> 00:49:35.243
And it's like, no,

00:49:35.243 --> 00:49:37.950
my story's gonna be the
true one about AI, etc.

00:49:37.950 --> 00:49:39.567
That part about, you
said the story about it

00:49:39.567 --> 00:49:42.221
and the meta narrative I
think is also of interest.

00:49:42.221 --> 00:49:43.350
Yeah.

00:49:43.350 --> 00:49:44.183
- Yeah.

00:49:44.183 --> 00:49:45.016
I think so.

00:49:45.016 --> 00:49:48.180
The very first PhD
student that I graduated

00:49:48.180 --> 00:49:52.680
named Jichen Zhu, her
PhD dissertation with me

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:56.817
was on what was called a
hermeneutics of AI systems.

00:49:56.817 --> 00:49:59.670
And so, basically, the interpretation,

00:49:59.670 --> 00:50:01.530
the experience of AI systems,

00:50:01.530 --> 00:50:03.330
and one of the things we spoke about

00:50:04.200 --> 00:50:07.920
and wrote about was it's not just a matter

00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:09.930
of the technical function of the system,

00:50:09.930 --> 00:50:12.540
we looked at other aspects such as,

00:50:12.540 --> 00:50:15.360
does it have human-like coherence?

00:50:15.360 --> 00:50:17.730
That was one criterion.

00:50:17.730 --> 00:50:19.950
Does it seem to have intentionality?

00:50:19.950 --> 00:50:23.040
Does it seem like it's about
the world within the world?

00:50:23.040 --> 00:50:23.873
Those sort of things.

00:50:23.873 --> 00:50:26.100
And so we had other types of criteria,

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:28.560
and then also the language
used to describe it.

00:50:28.560 --> 00:50:30.360
So if you have

00:50:30.360 --> 00:50:32.310
an algorithm

00:50:32.310 --> 00:50:35.820
that is described as
something like a combination

00:50:35.820 --> 00:50:38.520
of several letters and a number,

00:50:38.520 --> 00:50:41.250
and then people don't,

00:50:41.250 --> 00:50:42.990
but people don't know...

00:50:42.990 --> 00:50:44.100
Some people know what it does,

00:50:44.100 --> 00:50:48.060
but a lot of people don't
know what the system does.

00:50:48.060 --> 00:50:49.170
If you have another system

00:50:49.170 --> 00:50:50.930
that you call a machine learning system,

00:50:50.930 --> 00:50:52.920
if you have another system you call

00:50:52.920 --> 00:50:54.510
a case-based reasoning system,

00:50:54.510 --> 00:50:56.520
and whatever names,

00:50:56.520 --> 00:50:57.480
some of those names

00:50:57.480 --> 00:51:00.330
make it sound like they're
doing something very human-like,

00:51:00.330 --> 00:51:03.060
and if you say it's
doing something like...

00:51:03.060 --> 00:51:05.280
Now we just call it it's
like a heuristic algorithm.

00:51:05.280 --> 00:51:06.113
It's a really simple.

00:51:06.113 --> 00:51:07.440
But like A star,

00:51:07.440 --> 00:51:10.650
that's one of the first
algorithms I learned in AI

00:51:10.650 --> 00:51:11.483
course.

00:51:11.483 --> 00:51:12.316
It's a

00:51:12.316 --> 00:51:13.410
search algorithm,

00:51:13.410 --> 00:51:15.540
or something like Eliza did.

00:51:15.540 --> 00:51:17.700
- Well, I mean, even search
sounds intelligent nowadays

00:51:17.700 --> 00:51:19.230
because it's like, "Oh, yeah.

00:51:19.230 --> 00:51:20.400
Google search, Bing search."

00:51:20.400 --> 00:51:21.237
No, no, this is...

00:51:21.237 --> 00:51:22.140
No, this is not this.

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:24.150
This is just like,
"Okay, we look down here,

00:51:24.150 --> 00:51:25.800
then we look down here,
then we look down here."

00:51:25.800 --> 00:51:26.633
Like that.

00:51:26.633 --> 00:51:27.466
- Yes.

00:51:27.466 --> 00:51:30.630
So what we looked at was also
the role of that language

00:51:30.630 --> 00:51:32.610
in creating

00:51:32.610 --> 00:51:34.410
the impression of AI systems.

00:51:34.410 --> 00:51:35.550
And we weren't just saying

00:51:35.550 --> 00:51:38.010
that it was something like a falsehood

00:51:38.010 --> 00:51:40.410
or it was misleading
to say learning system

00:51:40.410 --> 00:51:41.760
or that sort of thing.

00:51:41.760 --> 00:51:44.400
But rather what role
does that language play?

00:51:44.400 --> 00:51:45.900
And so what we found was

00:51:45.900 --> 00:51:49.410
by analyzing the papers people
wrote about the systems,

00:51:49.410 --> 00:51:51.210
technical, published papers,

00:51:51.210 --> 00:51:52.230
there were some points

00:51:52.230 --> 00:51:54.780
where people would just
describe the algorithm,

00:51:54.780 --> 00:51:56.610
other points where people would say things

00:51:56.610 --> 00:51:59.730
like learning system and
these sort of things.

00:51:59.730 --> 00:52:01.770
And it might have helped
people to understand

00:52:01.770 --> 00:52:03.270
what their objectives were better,

00:52:03.270 --> 00:52:04.590
what they were trying to accomplish,

00:52:04.590 --> 00:52:05.520
how it could be used.

00:52:05.520 --> 00:52:06.353
And those sort of...

00:52:06.353 --> 00:52:08.280
I don't think they were
just trying to fool people

00:52:08.280 --> 00:52:09.113
to say like,

00:52:09.113 --> 00:52:10.980
"Well, this algorithm is
learning like a human child.",

00:52:10.980 --> 00:52:12.390
or something like that.

00:52:12.390 --> 00:52:16.470
But it goes to show
that the story about it,

00:52:16.470 --> 00:52:17.970
even in very technical work,

00:52:17.970 --> 00:52:21.720
plays a huge role in how
people understand the work.

00:52:21.720 --> 00:52:22.553
And yeah.

00:52:22.553 --> 00:52:26.940
So I think not just for
these extreme examples,

00:52:26.940 --> 00:52:30.150
but even for everyday
practice of technical AI,

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:32.040
the story that's being told plays a role.

00:52:32.040 --> 00:52:33.600
And it's important to be
able to tell that story

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:35.910
in a way that's understood
by the community.

00:52:35.910 --> 00:52:37.020
- There's a

00:52:37.020 --> 00:52:38.670
former member of the Italian parliament

00:52:38.670 --> 00:52:42.600
who suggested that instead of
AI, artificial intelligence,

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:43.960
you call these systems

00:52:45.030 --> 00:52:47.550
systematic approaches to

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:50.250
learning and machine inferences.

00:52:50.250 --> 00:52:52.650
And so you'd be asking questions like

00:52:52.650 --> 00:52:54.679
can this salami write rap?

00:52:54.679 --> 00:52:56.580
(laughs)

00:52:56.580 --> 00:52:58.859
Should this salami be given human rights?

00:52:58.859 --> 00:53:01.026
(laughs)

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:04.170
The meat of (indistinct)
(laughs)

00:53:04.170 --> 00:53:05.133
Yeah, exactly.

00:53:06.180 --> 00:53:07.710
So I think that's true,

00:53:07.710 --> 00:53:09.933
the naming, the framework,

00:53:11.700 --> 00:53:13.563
these are important issues,

00:53:16.757 --> 00:53:18.690
and also being aware that

00:53:18.690 --> 00:53:19.710
there's certain things.

00:53:19.710 --> 00:53:20.543
It's like

00:53:21.990 --> 00:53:24.490
if you just have a
probability distribution

00:53:25.560 --> 00:53:27.420
over sequences of words

00:53:27.420 --> 00:53:29.250
then it can do amazing things

00:53:29.250 --> 00:53:31.350
that we didn't realize it could do.

00:53:31.350 --> 00:53:33.630
And very coherent, very amazing outcomes.

00:53:33.630 --> 00:53:34.463
But

00:53:35.700 --> 00:53:36.764
not everything.

00:53:36.764 --> 00:53:39.450
(laughs)

00:53:39.450 --> 00:53:43.530
When I think about people who
want to engage with computing

00:53:43.530 --> 00:53:45.363
and do things in creative ways,

00:53:46.680 --> 00:53:49.380
a big piece of my advice is
still learn how to program

00:53:50.610 --> 00:53:52.470
because the prompt engineering,

00:53:52.470 --> 00:53:56.730
the idea that your job is gonna be done by

00:53:56.730 --> 00:53:57.870
emerging technologies,

00:53:57.870 --> 00:54:01.473
and you don't have to know
explicitly how computing works,

00:54:02.550 --> 00:54:05.580
as we've been talking about, actually,

00:54:05.580 --> 00:54:07.980
you're probably gonna be taking
some different approaches.

00:54:07.980 --> 00:54:09.210
You're probably gonna be

00:54:09.210 --> 00:54:11.400
wanting to do certain things explicitly.

00:54:11.400 --> 00:54:12.450
There's people who are writing

00:54:12.450 --> 00:54:14.100
very small processing programs

00:54:14.100 --> 00:54:16.860
to do really interesting visual sketches,

00:54:16.860 --> 00:54:18.990
there's people who are

00:54:18.990 --> 00:54:20.610
doing other things with AI,

00:54:20.610 --> 00:54:23.260
but you want to have the
range of those capabilities.

00:54:24.180 --> 00:54:25.440
- The question we just got

00:54:25.440 --> 00:54:29.460
was what kind of advice
would we leave people with?

00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:34.460
And to the extent that with
these emerging developments,

00:54:34.470 --> 00:54:36.990
I'm the person to give advice.

00:54:36.990 --> 00:54:38.370
I would say this,

00:54:38.370 --> 00:54:42.990
that when thinking about
technologies such as

00:54:42.990 --> 00:54:46.260
AI for creative practice
and developing artwork,

00:54:46.260 --> 00:54:48.030
creative cultural productions,

00:54:48.030 --> 00:54:50.103
there are two criteria
that people often have

00:54:50.103 --> 00:54:52.380
that I don't think are necessary.

00:54:52.380 --> 00:54:54.540
One of these is the idea

00:54:54.540 --> 00:54:57.150
that we have to make
the output human-like,

00:54:57.150 --> 00:55:02.150
and that's what Nick was
mentioning just a bit earlier.

00:55:02.310 --> 00:55:07.050
And I think we can do
all sorts of creative

00:55:07.050 --> 00:55:08.460
generative

00:55:08.460 --> 00:55:09.293
works,

00:55:09.293 --> 00:55:11.190
produce all types of system

00:55:11.190 --> 00:55:15.690
that don't have that kind
of criterion as end goal.

00:55:15.690 --> 00:55:17.910
The other thing that people tend to assume

00:55:17.910 --> 00:55:21.180
is that we often need
fully autonomous systems.

00:55:21.180 --> 00:55:23.320
I think we can instead think about systems

00:55:24.757 --> 00:55:26.610
that work collaboratively with us,

00:55:26.610 --> 00:55:28.710
that work in improvisational ways,

00:55:28.710 --> 00:55:32.220
that work in response to
broader cultural trends

00:55:32.220 --> 00:55:35.400
to what's bubbling up online and more.

00:55:35.400 --> 00:55:38.460
And I think we'll come up
with a lot of creative ideas

00:55:38.460 --> 00:55:41.970
and possibilities when
we think about AI systems

00:55:41.970 --> 00:55:44.370
in a way that relaxes
those kind of constraints.

00:55:46.800 --> 00:55:48.660
- And my advice,

00:55:48.660 --> 00:55:49.923
probably a little bit,

00:55:53.250 --> 00:55:54.083
what's the word for it?

00:55:54.083 --> 00:55:55.500
It's probably-
- Contrary?

00:55:55.500 --> 00:55:57.090
- Not even contrary.
(laughs)

00:55:57.090 --> 00:55:58.593
It's deeper than contrary.

00:55:59.700 --> 00:56:00.603
Combative.

00:56:01.590 --> 00:56:02.423
- Rebarbative.

00:56:04.620 --> 00:56:07.650
- Reductionist, extremely reductionist is

00:56:07.650 --> 00:56:08.483
don't use it.

00:56:10.842 --> 00:56:13.009
(laughs)

00:56:21.630 --> 00:56:23.430
To your point about programming,

00:56:23.430 --> 00:56:26.013
learn how to program, learn how to write,

00:56:27.300 --> 00:56:28.685
learn

00:56:28.685 --> 00:56:30.900
the programming that is

00:56:30.900 --> 00:56:34.380
traditional, classic, or
contemporary writing styles

00:56:34.380 --> 00:56:35.583
or art styles.

00:56:36.690 --> 00:56:40.500
There's still a lot of
opportunity for innovation

00:56:40.500 --> 00:56:43.650
on just classical, analog things

00:56:43.650 --> 00:56:47.790
that actually, I think,
AI and generative models

00:56:47.790 --> 00:56:49.020
will shed light on,

00:56:49.020 --> 00:56:52.650
which will show you things
that were in the shadows

00:56:52.650 --> 00:56:57.270
that once the light in
the sun that is AI shines,

00:56:57.270 --> 00:56:59.760
is gonna shed light on these
very classical traditions,

00:56:59.760 --> 00:57:02.310
these very analog traditions

00:57:02.310 --> 00:57:05.310
that still will generate things of value,

00:57:05.310 --> 00:57:06.610
cultural products of value

00:57:07.590 --> 00:57:08.950
and new skill sets

00:57:09.840 --> 00:57:11.400
to be developed and deployed.

00:57:11.400 --> 00:57:14.180
So I think there is
something to embracing in it

00:57:14.180 --> 00:57:17.190
in a collaborative way, to
understanding it in a deeper,

00:57:17.190 --> 00:57:19.590
below the surface type of way,

00:57:19.590 --> 00:57:22.540
but also focusing back on the human

00:57:23.730 --> 00:57:25.440
aspects and the human skill sets,

00:57:25.440 --> 00:57:28.080
and still doing the work
of the traditional schools

00:57:28.080 --> 00:57:30.030
and the formal practices
and things of that nature

00:57:30.030 --> 00:57:32.943
from a computational
side to an artistic side.

00:57:34.770 --> 00:57:36.183
That would be my advice.

